    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old male is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old girl presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old lady presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Scarlet Fever", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old woman is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old lady presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Styloglossus muscle", "Masseter muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Peritonsillar Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Pleural cavity", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Submandibular space"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old female is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old male presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Eikenella corrodens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old boy presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old lady is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy", "Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months", "Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s edema"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s Edema", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Adenovirus type 7"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old woman complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Intubation web", "Vocal cord nodule", "Glottic carcinoma", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord polyp"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old girl with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old male presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old man presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Arytenoideus muscle", "Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old boy complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old woman complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Branchial cleft cyst", "Acute suppurative parotitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Haemophilus influenzae", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Mumps paramyxovirus", "Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old male presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Parotid abscess", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sjogren''s syndrome", "Warthin''s tumor", "Oncocytoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old male presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths", "Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Trans-coelomic seeding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy", "Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old gentleman presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["Second branchial cleft cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "First branchial cleft cyst", "Thymic duct cyst"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old boy presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Lymphoma", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old girl presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old woman is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old boy presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Age between 3 and 14 years", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough", "Tonsillar exudates or swelling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old man presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Scarlet Fever", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old gentleman is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old gentleman presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Masseter muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Styloglossus muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Retropharyngeal Abscess", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Submandibular space", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old gentleman with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old lady is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament", "Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old female presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Eikenella corrodens"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old female presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old boy is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old girl presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage", "Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months", "Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old gentleman complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord nodules", "Vocal cord polyp", "Reinke''s edema", "Laryngeal papillomatosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old girl with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Contact granuloma", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Vocal cord nodules", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Reinke''s Edema"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Adenovirus type 7", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old male complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Intubation web", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord polyp", "Glottic carcinoma", "Vocal cord nodule"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old woman with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old girl presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old boy presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing", "Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy", "Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Arytenoideus muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old girl complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy", "Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old boy complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct", "Acute suppurative parotitis", "Branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Haemophilus influenzae", "Mumps paramyxovirus", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old gentleman presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Parotid abscess", "Warthin''s tumor", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sjogren''s syndrome", "Warthin''s tumor", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Oncocytoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old male presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Trans-coelomic seeding", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy", "Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old lady presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["Thymic duct cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "First branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old gentleman presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery", "Lymphoma", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Second branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old male is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old gentleman presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Age between 3 and 14 years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old female presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Scarlet Fever", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old female presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old boy presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Masseter muscle", "Styloglossus muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Peritonsillar Abscess", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Submandibular space", "Pleural cavity", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old female with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old lady is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old girl complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Eikenella corrodens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old female presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old girl is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old female presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy", "Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months", "Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old male complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Reinke''s edema", "Vocal cord nodules", "Vocal cord polyp", "Contact granuloma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old girl with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s Edema"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Adenovirus type 7", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old man complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Intubation web", "Vocal cord nodule", "Vocal cord polyp", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old lady with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old girl presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old female presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle", "Arytenoideus muscle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old male complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old lady complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute suppurative parotitis", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct", "Branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Mumps paramyxovirus", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Haemophilus influenzae", "Fusobacterium necrophorum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old girl presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Parotid abscess", "Warthin''s tumor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Oncocytoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Sjogren''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old lady presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Trans-coelomic seeding", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old boy presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["First branchial cleft cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "Thymic duct cyst", "Second branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old male presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Lymphoma", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery", "Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old lady presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old boy is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old girl presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old man presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Scarlet Fever", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old girl is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Strict bed rest for 2 months"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old girl presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Styloglossus muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Masseter muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Internal pterygoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Retropharyngeal Abscess"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Pleural cavity", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Submandibular space"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old lady with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old lady is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old gentleman presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Eikenella corrodens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old female presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old girl is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old woman presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Reinke''s edema", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord polyp", "Vocal cord nodules"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old woman with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Reinke''s Edema", "Vocal cord nodules", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Adenovirus type 7", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old lady complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Intubation web", "Vocal cord nodule", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old girl with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old lady presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal", "Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old gentleman presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Arytenoideus muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old boy complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Acute suppurative parotitis", "Branchial cleft cyst", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Mumps paramyxovirus", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Haemophilus influenzae"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old girl presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Parotid abscess", "Warthin''s tumor", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Oncocytoma", "Sjogren''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old female presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Trans-coelomic seeding", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Direct local invasion of the mandible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old gentleman presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["Thymic duct cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "First branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old gentleman presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Lymphoma", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old woman is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old female presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old woman presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Scarlet Fever", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old man is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old lady presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old female presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Styloglossus muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Masseter muscle"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Retropharyngeal Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Submandibular space", "Pleural cavity", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old lady with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old female is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst", "Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament", "Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old gentleman presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Eikenella corrodens", "Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old male presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old girl is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old man presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Systemic corticosteroid therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old male complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Vocal cord nodules", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Reinke''s edema", "Contact granuloma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old boy with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s Edema", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Adenovirus type 7", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord nodule", "Glottic carcinoma", "Vocal cord polyp", "Intubation web", "Contact granuloma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old woman with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old female presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old lady presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle", "Arytenoideus muscle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old woman complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old girl complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Branchial cleft cyst", "Acute suppurative parotitis", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Haemophilus influenzae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Mumps paramyxovirus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old female presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Parotid abscess", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Warthin''s tumor", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Sjogren''s syndrome", "Oncocytoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old male presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Trans-coelomic seeding", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Direct local invasion of the mandible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old woman presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["Cystic hygroma", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Thymic duct cyst", "First branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old lady presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery", "Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old lady is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old lady presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old man presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Scarlet Fever"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old woman is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old female presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old man presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Masseter muscle", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Styloglossus muscle"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Submandibular space", "Pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old boy with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old girl is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old female presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Streptococcus pyogenes", "Eikenella corrodens", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old woman presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old gentleman is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old girl presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months", "Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old man complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Vocal cord nodules", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Reinke''s edema", "Contact granuloma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old lady with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Contact granuloma", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s Edema"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Adenovirus type 7", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old woman complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Glottic carcinoma", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord nodule", "Intubation web"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old girl with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old male presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old woman presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle", "Arytenoideus muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old girl complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old girl complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute suppurative parotitis", "Branchial cleft cyst", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Mumps paramyxovirus", "Haemophilus influenzae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old boy presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Parotid abscess", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Oncocytoma", "Sjogren''s syndrome", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old lady presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Trans-coelomic seeding", "Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old female presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["Thymic duct cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "First branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)", "Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old girl presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Second branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery", "Lymphoma", "Cervical lymph node metastasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old lady presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old male is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old gentleman presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old female presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Scarlet Fever", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old girl is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months", "Strict bed rest for 2 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old boy presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old boy presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Masseter muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule", "Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Styloglossus muscle", "Internal pterygoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Submandibular space", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Pleural cavity", "Epidural space of the cervical spine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old girl with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia", "Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old gentleman is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old gentleman complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old boy presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Eikenella corrodens", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old man presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old girl is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old female presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy", "Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)", "Systemic corticosteroid therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old lady complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal cord nodules", "Reinke''s edema", "Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord polyp"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old girl with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Reinke''s Edema", "Vocal cord nodules", "Contact granuloma", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Laryngeal papillomatosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Adenovirus type 7", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old male complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord nodule", "Contact granuloma", "Intubation web", "Glottic carcinoma", "Vocal cord polyp"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old lady with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old male presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old man presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant", "Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'During laryngoscopy, the clinician wishes to assess the sole abductor muscle of the vocal cords. Which muscle is the only abductor of the true vocal cords?', '["Cricothyroid muscle", "Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle", "Thyroarytenoid muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Arytenoideus muscle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle is the ONLY abductor of the vocal cords (it rotates the arytenoids laterally to open the glottis). All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles act as adductors (closing the cords) or tensors.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old gentleman complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old male complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct", "Branchial cleft cyst", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Acute suppurative parotitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 78-year-old post-operative patient who has been nil-per-os (NPO) and on morphine since a hip replacement 4 days ago develops severe pain and swelling of the right cheek. Physical exam shows exquisite tenderness of the right parotid gland and purulent discharge from Stensen''s duct. What is the most common causative pathogen?', '["Haemophilus influenzae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Mumps paramyxovirus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Acute Suppurative Parotitis, classically seen in elderly, dehydrated, post-operative, or debilitated patients who are NPO. Reduced salivary flow allows retrograde migration of oral bacteria up Stensen''s duct into the parotid gland. The most common pathogen isolated is Staphylococcus aureus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old lady presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Warthin''s tumor", "Parotid abscess", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Oncocytoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Sjogren''s syndrome", "Warthin''s tumor", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old boy presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes", "Trans-coelomic seeding", "Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 10-year-old child presents with a painless, smooth, fluctuant midline neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. On examination, the mass is observed to move upward when the child swallows and when they protrude their tongue. What embryological remnant is responsible, and what is the definitive surgery?', '["Thyroglossal duct remnant; Sistrunk procedure (excision of cyst, tract, and central hyoid bone)", "Cystic hygroma; sclerotherapy with bleomycin", "Thyroid dysgenesis; total thyroidectomy", "Second branchial cleft remnant; simple surgical excision of the cyst", "First branchial cleft remnant; superficial parotidectomy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst, a congenital midline mass arising from the persistent tract of the descending thyroid gland from the foramen cecum. The pathognomonic sign is elevation upon tongue protrusion or swallowing due to its attachment to the hyoid bone. The definitive treatment is the Sistrunk Procedure, which requires excision of the cyst, the entire tract, and the central body of the hyoid bone to minimize recurrence.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old boy presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["First branchial cleft cyst", "Thymic duct cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Cystic hygroma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old woman presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery", "Lymphoma", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Cervical lymph node metastasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old male presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old lady is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old lady presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Age between 3 and 14 years", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)", "Tonsillar exudates or swelling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old girl presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Scarlet Fever", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old gentleman is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old male presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Styloglossus muscle", "Masseter muscle", "Internal pterygoid muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute Epiglottitis", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess", "Peritonsillar Abscess", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Submandibular space", "Epidural space of the cervical spine", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Pleural cavity", "Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old girl with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old gentleman is diagnosed with Ludwig''s Angina. What is the most common anatomical source of this infection?', '["Deep laceration of the posterior pharyngeal wall", "Suppurative palatine tonsillitis", "Acute sialadenitis of the parotid gland", "Infection of the mandibular second or third molars", "Erosion of a branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ludwig''s Angina is most frequently odontogenic in origin (~70-90% of cases), specifically arising from infections of the second and third mandibular molars. The roots of these teeth extend below the mylohyoid line, allowing infection to spread directly into the submandibular space.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old man complains of chronic unilateral throat pain, a sensation of a foreign body in the throat (globus), and referred ear pain that is characteristically exacerbated by head rotation. The patient had a tonsillectomy 5 years ago. Palpation of the tonsillar fossa reproduces the exact pain. What is the diagnostic finding on CT imaging?', '["Diverticular pouch originating from the pyriform sinus", "Elongated styloid process (>30 mm) and calcification of the stylohyoid ligament", "Avascular necrosis of the hyoid bone", "Erosion of the glossopharyngeal nerve by a posterior skull base tumor", "Thrombosis of the internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Eagle''s Syndrome, which is caused by an elongated styloid process (>30 mm) or calcification of the stylohyoid ligament. Post-tonsillectomy tissue scarring can tether local nerves (particularly the glossopharyngeal nerve) against the rigid styloid process, causing unilateral throat pain, globus sensation, and referred otalgia worsened by swallowing or turning the head.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old boy presents with severe neck pain, high fevers, and pleuritic chest pain one week after recovering from a mild sore throat. Physical exam shows tenderness and induration along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. CT of the neck reveals internal jugular vein (IJV) thrombophlebitis, and chest X-ray shows multiple cavitary nodules. What is the classic causative pathogen?', '["Eikenella corrodens", "Fusobacterium necrophorum", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Lemierre''s Syndrome (post-anginal septicemia), characterized by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein following a recent oropharyngeal infection, with subsequent metastatic septic emboli (typically to the lungs). The classic, highly associated causative pathogen is Fusobacterium necrophorum, an anaerobic gram-negative rod.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old lady presents with a 2-year history of progressive dysphagia, severe halitosis, and recurrent regurgitation of undigested food eaten hours prior. Occasionally, the patient experiences nocturnal coughing and choking episodes. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Traction diverticulum from mediastinal lymph node tuberculosis", "Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax (achalasia)", "Herniation of mucosa through Killian''s triangle due to cricopharyngeal incoordination", "Congenital duplication cyst of the upper esophagus", "Autoimmune destruction of the esophageal mucosal lining"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has a Zenker''s Diverticulum, a posterior pharyngeal pulsion diverticulum (a ''false'' diverticulum containing only mucosa and submucosa). It occurs at Killian''s Triangle, a site of natural weakness between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus muscle and the transverse fibers of the cricopharyngeus muscle. It is caused by chronic elevated intraluminal pressure, usually due to cricopharyngeal spasm or incoordination.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old male is suspected of having a Zenker''s Diverticulum based on clinical symptoms. Which of the following represents the diagnostic test of choice, and what procedure is strictly avoided/cautioned?', '["MRI of the neck is diagnostic; ultrasound is cautioned", "CT scan with oral contrast is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned", "Upper endoscopy is diagnostic; CT scan is cautioned due to radiation risk", "Barium swallow is diagnostic; upper endoscopy is cautioned due to perforation risk", "Esophageal manometry is diagnostic; barium swallow is cautioned"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A barium swallow (videofluoroscopy) is the gold standard diagnostic test for Zenker''s Diverticulum as it safely demonstrates the pouch. Upper endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) must be performed with extreme caution, as the endoscope can easily slip into the blind pouch and cause direct instrumental perforation of the thin wall.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old lady presents with rapid onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, and difficulty breathing. The patient is sitting in a ''tripod'' position (leaning forward, hands on knees, neck hyperextended) and has inspiratory stridor. No cough is heard. Lateral neck X-ray shows a swollen epiglottis (''thumbprint sign''). What is the most immediate priority?', '["Securing the airway in a controlled environment (operating room) with ENT and anesthesia present", "Intravenous administration of Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone", "Obtaining an urgent contrast CT scan of the neck to rule out abscess", "Taking a throat swab for culture and sensitivity in the emergency room", "Applying a tight-fitting oxygen mask and placing the patient supine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of Acute Epiglottitis, a medical emergency. The primary concern is sudden, complete airway obstruction. The absolute priority is securing the airway under controlled conditions (preferably in the operating room with a surgical airway cart standby). Any distressing procedures (such as throat examination, IV placement, or placing the patient supine) must be strictly avoided until the airway is secure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 2-month-old infant is brought to the clinic due to a high-pitched inspiratory stridor that is louder when the baby is supine or crying, and improves when prone. The baby is feeding well and gaining weight appropriately. Flexible laryngoscopy shows an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis that collapses into the laryngeal inlet during inspiration. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids", "Urgent referral for adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy", "Tracheostomy placement and long-term mechanical ventilation", "Immediate surgical supraglottoplasty to prevent airway damage", "Reassurance and observation, as most cases resolve spontaneously by 18-24 months"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of congenital stridor, characterized by flaccidity of the supraglottic structures. The classic findings are an ''omega-shaped'' epiglottis and inspiratory collapse. In mild-to-moderate cases where the infant is thriving, feeding normally, and not hypoxic, the treatment is supportive reassurance and observation, as it typically resolves spontaneously by 18-24 months of age as laryngeal cartilage matures.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old singer presents with a 6-month history of progressive hoarseness. Flexible laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, symmetric, small, well-demarcated fibrous nodules at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the true vocal cords. What is the first-line treatment?', '["Micro-laryngeal surgery (laser excision)", "Systemic corticosteroid therapy", "Bilateral vocal cord steroid injections", "Long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy", "Speech therapy (voice hygiene and behavioral therapy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Vocal Cord Nodules (''singer''s nodes''), which are bilateral, symmetric lesions caused by chronic vocal abuse or phonotrauma. They characteristically form at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal cords (the point of maximum vibratory impact). First-line therapy is conservative speech/voice therapy to correct vocal mechanics. Surgery is reserved only for refractory cases in adults.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old girl complains of sudden-onset hoarseness after screaming loudly at a sporting event last weekend. Laryngoscopy reveals a unilateral, pedunculated, fluid-filled translucent lesion on the left true vocal cord. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal cord polyp", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal cord nodules", "Contact granuloma", "Reinke''s edema"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A vocal cord polyp is typically a unilateral, pedunculated or sessile lesion that often arises after a single episode of acute vocal trauma (causing local capillary hemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria). This contrasts with vocal cord nodules, which are always bilateral and result from chronic vocal abuse.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old gentleman with a 45-pack-year smoking history presents with a progressive, painless lowering of their voice pitch (''smoker''s voice''). Laryngoscopy reveals bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous, ''jelly-like'' swelling of the superficial lamina propria of both true vocal cords. What is the diagnosis?', '["Reinke''s Edema", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Vocal cord nodules", "Contact granuloma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reinke''s Edema is a bilateral, diffuse, gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space) of the vocal cords. It is strongly associated with chronic smoking and vocal abuse, presenting classically as a very low-pitched, gravelly voice, particularly in older women.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old child presents with progressive hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. Laryngoscopy reveals multiple ''raspberry-like'', exophytic, warty growths on the true vocal cords and epiglottis. Biopsy shows benign squamous papillomas. What are the most common causative viral strains?', '["Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 16 and 18", "Adenovirus type 7", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Cytomegalovirus (CMV)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis (RRP), a benign but aggressive condition caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. It is acquired via vertical transmission during childbirth from maternal vaginal condylomas. It is characterized by frequent recurrences requiring multiple surgical debulking procedures to maintain airway patency.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old male complains of throat discomfort and a ''catching'' sensation when speaking. The patient has a history of being intubated in the ICU for 6 days following a major trauma 2 months ago. Laryngoscopy shows a reddish-gray mass on the medial surface of the vocal process of the left arytenoid cartilage. What is the diagnosis?', '["Contact granuloma", "Vocal cord polyp", "Vocal cord nodule", "Glottic carcinoma", "Intubation web"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a Contact Granuloma (intubation granuloma), which typically forms on the posterior third of the vocal cords (the vocal process of the arytenoid cartilage). This is where the endotracheal tube rests during intubation, leading to mucosal erosion and subsequent granuloma formation. They can also be caused by gastroesophageal reflux and severe throat clearing.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old lady with a heavy smoking history presents with persistent hoarseness for 3 months. There is no dysphagia or weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, white, leukoplakic lesion on the middle third of the right true vocal cord. Vocal cord mobility is completely normal. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. What is the lymphatic staging implication?', '["Immediate systemic chemotherapy is indicated for distant micro-metastases", "High risk of bilateral cervical nodal metastasis due to rich glottic lymphatics", "Very low risk of early nodal metastasis due to the scant lymphatic drainage of the true vocal cords", "Occult nodal rate is greater than 30%", "Mandatory bilateral neck dissection is required"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an early (T1) Glottic Cancer. The true vocal cords (glottis) have virtually absent (scant) lymphatic drainage. Therefore, T1 glottic squamous cell carcinoma carries an extremely low risk of cervical lymph node metastasis (<1%). Consequently, neck dissection is not indicated for early glottic cancer, and local therapy (radiation or laser cordectomy) yields cure rates >90-95%.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old female presents with a persistent ''lump in the throat'', painful swallowing (dysphagia), referred ear pain (otalgia), and weight loss. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, ulcerated mass on the epiglottis. Biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. What is the primary pathway for the referred ear pain?', '["Direct tumor invasion into the Eustachian tube", "Referred pain via the Trigeminal nerve (auriculotemporal branch)", "Referred pain via the Vagus nerve (Arnold''s branch) and Glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s branch)", "Secondary middle ear effusion due to lymphatic backup", "Tumor obstruction of the external auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Referred otalgia in supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer is a classic red flag. The Vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory innervation to the larynx (internal laryngeal nerve) and also has an auricular branch (Arnold''s nerve) supplying the ear canal. The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) also participates via its tympanic branch (Jacobson''s nerve) from the pharynx. Irritation of CN IX or X by the tumor is referred to the ear.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old female presents with a weak, ''breathy'' voice and frequent coughing when drinking liquids following a recent total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy reveals that the left vocal cord is fixed in an abducted (lateral) position. What nerve was injured, and what is the goal of medialization thyroplasty?', '["Left Superior Laryngeal Nerve; to restore tensing capability of the cricothyroid muscle", "Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve; to push the paralyzed cord to the midline so the mobile right cord can approximate it", "Ansa Cervicalis; to reinnervate the lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves; to widen the glottic aperture for breathing", "Left Vagus Nerve; to bypass the motor pathway using an electrical implant"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) provides motor supply to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Injury (commonly during thyroidectomy) leads to vocal cord paralysis. Unilateral paralysis causes a breathy voice and aspiration of liquids due to incomplete glottic closure. Medialization thyroplasty (Type I) surgically shifts the paralyzed cord medially so the functioning opposite cord can achieve closure.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A patient develops immediate severe inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room after a total thyroidectomy. Surprisingly, the patient''s voice is near-normal. Laryngoscopy shows both vocal cords are fixed in the midline position. What is the explanation and immediate management?', '["Tracheal compression from hematoma; immediate opening of the neck incision", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; emergency airway management (re-intubation or tracheostomy)", "Post-operative hypocalcemia causing laryngeal spasm; oral calcium supplementation", "Bilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve injury; administration of high-dose IV calcium", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve injury; urgent speech therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) injury is a catastrophic surgical complication. Because the vocal cords lose all abductor function (Posterior Cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor), they drift into the midline (paramedian) position. While this allows near-normal voice production (since they are close together), it severely compromises the airway, causing immediate post-operative stridor. Emergency airway protection is mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old lady complains of a persistent globus sensation (''lump in the throat''), chronic throat clearing, and morning hoarseness. The patient denies having any heartburn or indigestion. Laryngoscopy reveals marked erythema of the arytenoids and thickening of the posterior commissure (''pachyderma''). What is the most appropriate management?', '["Surgical Nissen fundoplication immediately", "Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy of the posterior commissure", "Empiric course of oral antibiotics for chronic laryngitis", "Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy twice daily and dietary modifications", "Standard once-daily H2-blocker therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR), also known as ''silent reflux'' because patients frequently lack classic gastroesophageal reflux symptoms (heartburn). Laryngoscopy typically shows posterior glottic inflammation (arytenoid erythema, pachyderma). First-line therapy requires twice-daily proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) for a prolonged course (3-6 months) along with anti-reflux dietary modifications.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old male complains of a painful swelling under the left jaw that occurs predictably at mealtimes and gradually subsides over the next 1-2 hours. Physical exam reveals a firm, tender mass in the left submandibular region. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute suppurative parotitis", "Branchial cleft cyst", "Sialolithiasis of Stensen''s duct", "Pleomorphic adenoma of the submandibular gland", "Sialolithiasis of Wharton''s duct"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Sialolithiasis (salivary duct stone) of Wharton''s duct, which drains the submandibular gland. Mealtime salivary stimulation causes saliva backup behind the obstructing stone, leading to acute, painful swelling that slowly resolves as saliva trickles out. The submandibular gland is the most common site (~80%) due to its viscous, alkaline saliva and tortuous, upward duct.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 65-year-old girl presents with a painless, slow-growing mass in front of their right ear. Physical exam reveals a 2.5 cm, firm, mobile, non-tender lobulated mass in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland. There is no facial weakness. MRI shows a well-demarcated heterogeneous mass. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Parotid abscess", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Warthin''s tumor", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pleomorphic Adenoma (benign mixed tumor) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (~80% of all salivary tumors, primarily occurring in the superficial lobe of the parotid gland). It is slow-growing, painless, firm, and mobile. Because of its irregular pseudopods and incomplete capsule, simple enucleation is associated with high recurrence rates; the standard treatment is superficial parotidectomy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 68-year-old male with a heavy smoking history presents with bilateral, painless, soft swellings at the angle of the jaw. Fine needle aspiration reveals oncocytes and a lymphoid stroma. Tc-99m scintigraphy demonstrates increased uptake (''hot spots'') in both parotid tails. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Sjogren''s syndrome", "Warthin''s tumor", "Oncocytoma", "Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Warthin''s Tumor (cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign parotid tumor. It is highly associated with male gender and smoking, and is bilateral/multifocal in 10% of cases. Histologically, it contains oncocytic epithelium and a lymphoid stroma. It is the only salivary tumor that concentrates Technetium-99m, appearing ''hot'' on nuclear scintigraphy.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old girl presents with a painful submandibular gland mass and numbness of the chin. Biopsy reveals a malignant tumor with a classic ''cribriform'' (Swiss-cheese) histological pattern. What is the primary route of spread for this neoplasm?', '["Early hematogenous spread to liver and brain", "Trans-coelomic seeding", "Perineural invasion and tracking along nerve sheaths", "Direct local invasion of the mandible", "Rapid lymphatic spread to bilateral cervical nodes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, a malignant salivary gland tumor. It is highly characterized by perineural invasion (tendency to track along nerve sheaths), which explains the presentation of pain and neurological symptoms (like numbness in the V3 distribution). Histologically, the ''cribriform'' or Swiss-cheese pattern is classic. It has a high late recurrence and distant metastasis rate (especially to the lungs).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old man presents with a painless lateral neck mass located along the anterior border of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle. The mass is soft and fluctuant. What is the most likely embryological origin?', '["First branchial cleft cyst", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Cystic hygroma", "Thymic duct cyst"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A Second Branchial Cleft Cyst is the most common branchial anomaly (~95%). It presents classically as a soft, fluctuant, painless mass along the anterior border of the upper SCM muscle, lateral to the carotid sheath. First branchial cleft cysts are located near the external auditory canal or angle of the jaw.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A newborn is noted to have a large, soft, compressible, and brilliantly transilluminating mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. What is the diagnosis, and what genetic syndrome is most commonly associated?', '["Hemangioma; Sturge-Weber syndrome", "Thyroglossal duct cyst; Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)", "Cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation); Turner syndrome (45,X)", "Branchial cleft cyst; DiGeorge syndrome", "Plunging ranula; Marfan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Cystic Hygroma is a congenital macrocystic lymphatic malformation, most commonly found in the posterior triangle of the neck. It is soft, compressible, and characteristically transilluminates brilliantly. It is highly associated with Turner Syndrome (45,X) and other aneuploidies (Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 12-year-old male presents with a slowly enlarging, painless neck mass at the level of the hyoid bone. Physical exam reveals a firm, pulsatile mass that can be moved horizontally but NOT vertically. Angiography reveals a splaying of the internal and external carotid arteries (''lyre sign''). What is the diagnosis?', '["Cervical lymph node metastasis", "Lymphoma", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Second branchial cleft cyst", "Aneurysm of the internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is a Carotid Body Tumor (chemodectoma/paraganglioma), a highly vascular neuroendocrine tumor located at the carotid bifurcation. Classic findings include a pulsatile mass with lateral but not vertical mobility (Fontaine''s Sign) and splaying of the ICA and ECA on angiography (the ''Lyre Sign''). Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated. Hereditary cases are associated with succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) mutations.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old gentleman presents with a 2-day history of severe sore throat, high fever, and painful swallowing. The patient does not have a cough or runny nose. Physical exam reveals beefy red tonsils with white exudates and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Obtain a throat culture and start Amoxicillin immediately", "Perform a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT)", "Prescribe oral Azithromycin", "Reassure the patient and recommend supportive care only", "Empirically initiate oral Penicillin V"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has a Centor score of 4 (fever, tonsillar exudates, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, absence of cough), which indicates a high probability of Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (GABHS) pharyngitis. Current guidelines recommend confirming the diagnosis with a Rapid Antigen Detection Test (RADT) or throat culture before initiating antibiotic therapy to avoid unnecessary use.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old boy is diagnosed with Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis. What is the primary medical reason for treating this patient with a full course of antibiotics?', '["To decrease the risk of secondary bacterial sinusitis", "To shorten the duration of symptomatic sore throat", "To prevent transmission of viral co-infections", "To prevent Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)", "To prevent the development of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The primary clinical driver for antibiotic therapy (such as Penicillin or Amoxicillin) in GABHS pharyngitis is the prevention of Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF). While antibiotics reduce the severity and duration of symptoms, they do NOT reliably prevent the development of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old gentleman presents with a sore throat, fever, and tonsillar exudates. The clinician calculates a Centor score. Which of the following is NOT a component of the Centor criteria?', '["Tonsillar exudates or swelling", "Tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Presence of rhinorrhea and cough", "Age between 3 and 14 years", "History of fever >38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Centor criteria (and its McIsaac modification) include: history of fever (1 point), tonsillar exudates or swelling (1 point), tender anterior cervical adenopathy (1 point), and absence of cough (1 point). A cough or runny nose (rhinorrhea) actually subtracts from the likelihood of a bacterial infection, as it points to a viral etiology.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old male presents with 2 weeks of progressive fatigue, severe sore throat, and swollen glands. The patient was given Amoxicillin at an urgent care clinic 3 days ago and has since developed a generalized, pruritic, maculopapular rash. Physical exam shows posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute retroviral syndrome (HIV)", "Type I immediate hypersensitivity to Penicillins", "Scarlet Fever", "Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic presentation of Infectious Mononucleosis caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV). When patients with EBV mono are mistakenly treated with aminopenicillins (such as Amoxicillin or Ampicillin), a characteristic non-allergic maculopapular rash develops in up to 90% of cases. Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly indicative of EBV.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old female is confirmed to have Infectious Mononucleosis. What is the most critical activity restriction that must be advised to this patient?', '["Isolation from household contacts for 10 days", "Avoidance of contact sports for at least 3-4 weeks", "Strict bed rest for 2 months", "Avoidance of dairy products and fatty foods", "Complete avoidance of physical exercise for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Splenic rupture is a rare but life-threatening complication of Infectious Mononucleosis due to transient splenomegaly. Patients must be strictly advised to avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 3 to 4 weeks from symptom onset, or until clinical resolution and confirmation that splenomegaly has resolved.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old male presents with a severe, progressive unilateral sore throat, high fever, and difficulty opening their mouth (trismus). The voice sounds muffled (''hot potato'' voice) and there is pooling of saliva. On exam, the right tonsil is bulging medially, pushing the uvula to the left. What is the most appropriate definitive management?', '["Immediate urgent bilateral tonsillectomy", "High-dose oral Amoxicillin and close outpatient observation", "Needle aspiration or incision and drainage (I&D) plus intravenous antibiotics", "Direct laryngoscopy in the operating room under general anesthesia", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical presentation (unilateral tonsillar bulge, uvular deviation to the opposite side, trismus, and muffled ''hot potato'' voice) is diagnostic of a Peritonsillar Abscess (PTA / Quinsy). The standard definitive management is drainage (either via needle aspiration or incision and drainage) combined with systemic antibiotics to cover mixed aerobes and anaerobes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 5-year-old female presents with trismus and a peritonsillar abscess. Irritation of which anatomical structure is responsible for the patient''s trismus?', '["Internal pterygoid muscle", "Masseter muscle", "Glossopharyngeal nerve", "Styloglossus muscle", "Temporomandibular joint capsule"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Trismus (difficulty opening the mouth) in a peritonsillar abscess is caused by inflammatory irritation and spasm of the adjacent internal (medial) pterygoid muscle, which lies close to the superior tonsillar pillar.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with high fever, neck stiffness, and refusal to swallow. The child is holding their head in a slightly extended position. Lateral neck X-ray reveals significant widening of the prevertebral soft tissue space. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Peritonsillar Abscess", "Cervical Spine Osteomyelitis", "Acute Epiglottitis", "Retropharyngeal Abscess", "Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Retropharyngeal abscess typically occurs in young children (under age 6) due to suppuration of lymph nodes in the retropharyngeal space (Nodes of Rouviere) that subsequently atrophy. Key findings include high fever, neck stiffness (often mistaken for meningitis), head extension, and prevertebral space widening on lateral X-ray.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A child is diagnosed with a retropharyngeal abscess. If untreated, this infection can spread directly downward into which anatomical compartment, causing life-threatening complications?', '["Posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space''", "Pleural cavity", "Anterior mediastinum via the pretracheal space", "Submandibular space", "Epidural space of the cervical spine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The retropharyngeal space communicates directly with the posterior mediastinum via the ''danger space'' (located between the alar fascia and prevertebral fascia). Uncontrolled retropharyngeal infections can track rapidly into the chest, causing highly lethal acute mediastinitis.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-throat' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old girl with a history of poor dental hygiene presents with rapid onset of bilateral submandibular swelling, severe mouth pain, and a ''woody'' induration of the neck. On examination, the floor of the mouth is highly elevated and erythematous, pushing the tongue upward and backward. The patient is drooling and tachypneic. What is the most crucial first step in management?', '["Immediate intravenous administration of high-dose Clindamycin", "Securing the airway (via awake fiberoptic intubation or tracheostomy)", "Incision and drainage of the submandibular space under local anesthesia", "Extracting the infected mandibular molars", "Obtaining an urgent contrast-enhanced CT scan of the neck"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Ludwig''s Angina, a rapidly progressive, life-threatening bilateral cellulitis of the sublingual, submandibular, and submental spaces. The primary cause of death is asphyxiation due to posterior displacement of the tongue obstructing the airway. The absolute first priority is securing the airway, preferably via awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway. CT scans or drainage must never delay airway protection.');
